# fedora-cloud.sh configuration — simple KEY=VALUE, one per line.
# Copy to fedora-cloud.conf and edit. Pass with: fedora-cloud.sh --config fedora-cloud.conf <cmd>
#
# Blank lines and '#' comments (whole-line or trailing/inline) are ignored, and
# surrounding whitespace is trimmed — so you can uncomment a line below and keep
# its trailing comment.
# Unknown keys or malformed values are rejected. Paths are LITERAL: '~' is NOT
# expanded — use an absolute path for any *_DIR / *_FILE.
#
# Most keys are optional (a commented default is shown). OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR and
# BASE_IMAGE_DIR have NO default and MUST be set (uncommented below):
#   OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR  required for build / boot / run
#   BASE_IMAGE_DIR     required for run (the image download target)

# --- REQUIRED: image directories (no defaults) ---
# Overlay + seed live here (the seed dir is per-DOMAIN, so several guests can
# safely share one OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR — give each a distinct DOMAIN):
#   <OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR>/<DOMAIN>.qcow2              the qcow2 overlay
#   <OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR>/build/<DOMAIN>/user-data    rendered cloud-config
#   <OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR>/build/<DOMAIN>/meta-data
#   <OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR>/build/<DOMAIN>/seed.iso     (only if cloud-localds is installed)
OVERLAY_IMAGE_DIR=/var/lib/fedora-cloud/overlays
# Downloaded base images + CHECKSUM + keyring live here (flat, no structure):
BASE_IMAGE_DIR=/var/lib/fedora-cloud/base

# --- seed / paths ---
# TEMPLATE=./user-data.yaml            # cloud-config template
# TMUX_CONF=./dotfiles/tmux.conf       # tmux config injected into the std user's ~/.tmux.conf
# DIAG_SCRIPT=./tools/guest-diagnostics.sh  # script injected when DIAGNOSTICS=yes (see below)
# KEYS_DIR=~/.ssh                      # where the SSH keys live (default $HOME/.ssh)
# STD_USER=appuser                     # standard (no-sudo) username; valid Linux name
# ADMIN_USER=admin                     # admin (wheel, passwordless sudo) username
#                                      # must differ from each other, and must not be a
#                                      # reserved/system account (root, fedora, default, ...)
# STD_KEY_FILE=~/.ssh/appuser-fedora-cloud-01.pub  # defaults to KEYS_DIR/<STD_USER>-<DOMAIN>.pub
# ADM_KEY_FILE=~/.ssh/admin-fedora-cloud-01.pub    # defaults to KEYS_DIR/<ADMIN_USER>-<DOMAIN>.pub
# GENERATE_KEYS=yes                    # if a key file is absent: yes = create it;
#                                      # no = fail (you must provide the key yourself)
# ENCRYPT_KEYS=yes                     # auto-generated keys: yes = passphrase prompt
#                                      # (interactive); no = -N '' (non-interactive)
# CRYPTO_POLICY=DEFAULT:NO-SHA1        # system-wide crypto policy set on the guest.
#                                      # DEFAULT:NO-SHA1 bans SHA-1 but allows 2048-bit
#                                      # RSA certs; FUTURE is stricter (3072-bit RSA) and
#                                      # can break TLS to some CDNs (e.g. the Claude installer).
# SEED_METHOD=seed-iso                 # how the cloud-init seed reaches the guest:
#                                      #   seed-iso   build a cidata ISO (needs cloud-localds)
#                                      #              and attach it as a CDROM (default)
#                                      #   cloud-init let virt-install build+attach its own seed
# INSTANCE_ID=fedora-01                # cloud-init instance-id  [A-Za-z0-9._-]
# VM_HOSTNAME=fedora-01                # guest hostname          [A-Za-z0-9._-]

# --- libvirt / boot ---
# DOMAIN=fedora-cloud-01               # libvirt domain name     [A-Za-z0-9._-]
# RAM_MB=2048                          # memory, positive integer
# VCPUS=2                              # vCPUs, positive integer
# LIBVIRT_URI=qemu:///session          # libvirt connection URI (unprivileged user session)
# NETWORK=user                         # virt-install --network. Options:
#                                      #   user            session-safe, no routable IP (default)
#                                      #   bridge=virbr0   session + bridge for a real IP (see README)
#                                      #   network=default only with a system URI: qemu:///system
#                                      # A virtio NIC is pinned unless you add ,model=... yourself.
# OSINFO=detect=on,require=off,name=fedora43  # virt-install --osinfo. --import can't detect the
#                                      # OS, so name= is the fallback (avoids the 'generic'
#                                      # perf warning). Use an id your osinfo-db has:
#                                      # `osinfo-query os | grep fedora`, or a generic linux2024.
# TPM=no                               # yes = attach an emulated TPM 2.0 (needs swtpm on the
#                                      # host). Makes systemd-tpm2-setup succeed + enables
#                                      # measured boot; default no (that unit fails harmlessly).
# OVMF_CODE=/usr/share/edk2/ovmf/OVMF_CODE.fd  # explicit UEFI firmware (optional)
# NVRAM_PATH=/var/lib/fedora-cloud/web01_VARS.fd  # per-VM UEFI varstore path (uefi/uefi-secure
#                                      # only; empty = libvirt's default nvram dir)

# --- firewall / Samba / fail2ban ---
# FIREWALL=yes                         # install + enable firewalld on the guest.
#                                      # Defaults to YES because the Fedora Cloud Base
#                                      # image ships with NO packet filter at all (its
#                                      # KIWI description carries <ignore name="firewalld"/>),
#                                      # so this closes a real hole. It costs nothing:
#                                      # firewalld's default `public` zone already allows
#                                      # SSH, and established connections survive both the
#                                      # start and any later reload.
#
# SAMBA=no                             # export the standard user's ~/projects over SMB3
#                                      # so the HOST can mount it. Requires:
#                                      #   * a network the host can reach — NETWORK=bridge=virbr0
#                                      #     (or network=default with qemu:///system). The default
#                                      #     NETWORK=user gives the guest NO routable address, so
#                                      #     the host cannot open a connection to it at all and
#                                      #     `build` will refuse.
#                                      #   * FIREWALL=yes — without it, port 445 is open to every
#                                      #     other guest on the bridge, not just your host.
#                                      # The guest's Unix accounts stay password-less: Samba
#                                      # authenticates from its own passdb (tdbsam) and never
#                                      # reads /etc/shadow.
# SMB_HOST_ADDR=192.168.122.1          # the HOST's address on the libvirt bridge — the only
#                                      # source allowed to reach port 445 (firewalld scopes it
#                                      # to /32, and smb.conf's `hosts allow` repeats it). A bare
#                                      # IPv4, not a CIDR. Check yours: `ip -4 addr show virbr0`.
# SMB_PASSWORD_FILE=~/.ssh/smb-appuser-fedora-cloud-01.cred
#                                      # defaults to KEYS_DIR/smb-<STD_USER>-<DOMAIN>.cred.
#                                      # Generated (0600) on the first `build` with SAMBA=yes, in
#                                      # mount.cifs's `credentials=` format — so the file that is
#                                      # created IS the file you mount with. Delete it to rotate.
#                                      # NOTE: the password is also injected into the seed, so it
#                                      # exists in plaintext in seed.iso and in the guest's
#                                      # /var/lib/cloud/instance/user-data.txt (0600 root). See the
#                                      # README — it is not a login credential (SSH is key-only).
#
# FAIL2BAN=no                          # install fail2ban with jails for sshd, Samba (only if
#                                      # SAMBA=yes) and recidive. Be clear-eyed about what this
#                                      # buys: with key-only SSH and 445 already scoped to one
#                                      # source IP, the firewall and the key policy are doing the
#                                      # real work. This is defence-in-depth and log hygiene.
# FAIL2BAN_IGNOREIP=127.0.0.1/8 ::1 192.168.122.0/24
#                                      # never ban these. Keep your own subnet in here — this is
#                                      # what stops fail2ban locking YOU out. Recover from the
#                                      # serial console: `fail2ban-client unban --all`.
#
# DIAGNOSTICS=no                       # drop the read-only guest-diagnostics.sh (DIAG_SCRIPT) into
#                                      # the admin user's home, so after boot you can run
#                                      # `sudo bash ~/guest-diagnostics.sh` to dump cloud-init /
#                                      # firewall / Samba / fail2ban state. A debugging aid; off by
#                                      # default so ordinary guests don't carry it.

# --- image + download / verification ---
# IMAGE_VARIANT=generic                # which Cloud Base image to fetch/boot:
#                                      #   generic  hybrid BIOS+UEFI, works with any MODE (default)
#                                      #   uki      UEFI-only (MODE must be uefi/uefi-secure)
#                                      # NOTE: MODE (bios|uefi|uefi-secure) is the VM *firmware*,
#                                      # passed to run/boot — it is independent of IMAGE_VARIANT.
# VER=44                               # Fedora release, positive integer
# ARCH=x86_64                          # x86_64 | aarch64 | ppc64le | s390x
# FEDORA_GPG_URL=https://fedoraproject.org/fedora.gpg   # https:// only
# CHECKSUM_URL=                        # https:// full CHECKSUM url (skips discovery)
# COMPOSE=1.5                          # compose id (N or N.N); builds the CHECKSUM name
